  "tagline": "Tiny C HTTP key-value server using LMDB for storage and H2O for HTTP, acking only after data is on disk.",
  "description": "PearlDB is a minimal key-value database server in C with a RESTful HTTP interface: PUT, GET and DELETE on keys, with persistent connections and pipelining. It hands storage to LMDB and HTTP handling to the H2O library, batching LMDB writes so that a successful HTTP response means the write is durably on disk, and because the API is plain HTTP a caching reverse proxy can sit in front to scale reads. Written by Willem Thiart as a deliberately small, readable codebase, it stopped at version 0.1.0 and its last commits date from January 2018. It is best used as a reference for wiring LMDB to an HTTP server rather than as a production store.",
